.TH ds-lite-punch 8  "ds-lite-punch 0.3.5" "" "Manual"
.SH NAME
ds\-lite\-punch \- CGNAT\-aware UDP relay for a ds\-lite line: keep the carrier\*(Aqs mapping alive and forward inbound traffic to a br\-lan target.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBds\-lite\-punch\fR \-\-static\-map R=ip:port [OPTIONS]
.br
\fBds\-lite\-punch\fR \-\-bind ip:port \-\-target ip:port [OPTIONS]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The daemon keeps one external mapping alive on a ds\-lite line by writing to it, learns the mapping\*(Aqs external address and port with STUN, and forwards inbound UDP and TCP to a target on the local network with the peer\*(Aqs source address preserved. It answers UPnP IGD, PCP and NAT\-PMP on the LAN, and it can report on the carrier\*(Aqs filtering from outside the line.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-static\-map\fR \fI<R=IP:PORT>\fR
Hold the mapping for slot port R, forwarding inbound datagrams to ip:port on the local network. Repeatable, one entry per instance. Either this or the legacy \-\-bind/\-\-target pair is required.
.TP
\fB\-\-bind\fR \fI<IP:PORT>\fR
Legacy single\-map form: the relay socket on the CGNAT\-facing address, which is the tuple the carrier maps. Requires \-\-target.
.TP
\fB\-\-target\fR \fI<IP:PORT>\fR
Legacy single\-map form: the local endpoint inbound datagrams are forwarded to, with the peer\*(Aqs source address preserved. Requires \-\-bind.
.TP
\fB\-\-stun\fR \fI<HOST:PORT,...>\fR
STUN servers, comma\-separated as host:port, tried in order and rotated when one falls silent. Default: stun.l.google.com:19302,stun.cloudflare.com:3478.
.TP
\fB\-\-interval\fR \fI<SECS>\fR
Seconds between the STUN writes that keep the mapping alive. The writes are what the carrier sees, so this is the interval the mapping\*(Aqs lifetime depends on. Default 2, minimum 1.
.TP
\fB\-\-gateway\fR \fI<IP>\fR
Next hop used to route STUN out the line the mapping is on. Without it the default route wins and STUN reports the wrong nat. Default 192.168.0.1.
.TP
\fB\-\-state\-dir\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Directory for the published tuple, the facade\*(Aqs identity and the DeviceProtection store. The lease records and the epoch are kept at their own fixed path, /tmp/dslp. Both directories are on temporary filesystems, so neither survives a reboot. Default /run/ds\-lite\-punch.
.TP
\fB\-\-slot\-port\-range\fR \fI<LO\-HI>\fR
Port range the slot engine allocates from, written LO\-HI. Ports in it must not overlap the relay socket. Default 30000\-39999.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-slots\fR \fI<N>\fR
Maximum concurrent slots. Default 32.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-maps\-per\-client\fR \fI<N>\fR
Maximum mappings per client. Default 16.
.TP
\fB\-\-gc\-grace\-factor\fR \fI<N>\fR
Grace multiple applied to a slot\*(Aqs lifetime before it is collected. Default 3.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-refresh\-attempts\fR \fI<N>\fR
The keepalive\*(Aqs budget. It bounds the refresh attempts for one flow whose conntrack entry has gone, and the number of flows kept alive at once. Default 8.
.TP
\fB\-\-observation\fR
Report the named devices\*(Aq live flows and touch nothing. This is the stage a new device is admitted from, before \-\-keepalive arms the arm.
.TP
\fB\-\-allowlist\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
File of IPv4 addresses, one per line, with # for comments. These are the devices the keepalive acts for. The list is a budget as well as an admission: a flow kept alive costs about half a packet a second at the default interval. The path is read at startup, so a typo fails the start.
.TP
\fB\-\-keepalive\fR
Keep the named devices\*(Aq mappings alive: the RFC conntrack lifetimes, plus the daemon\*(Aqs own writes on the device\*(Aqs behalf. Without this flag the allowlist is reported on and nothing is touched.
.TP
\fB\-\-cdc\fR \fI<proc|nft|aya>\fR
How conntrack entries are removed: proc, nft, or aya. The nft backend is the default and the one this line is measured with.
.TP
\fB\-\-pcp\fR
Answer PCP (RFC 6887) and NAT\-PMP (RFC 6886) on UDP 5351, on the local network only, using the same slot engine as the UPnP facade. Off by default.
.TP
\fB\-\-pcp\-peer\fR
Answer the PCP PEER opcode with the mapping\*(Aqs own tuple. The datapath is endpoint\-independent, so that opcode installs nothing and is otherwise refused.
.TP
\fB\-\-upnp\-port\fR \fI<N>\fR
Port the UPnP IGD facade serves on. Default 49152.
.TP
\fB\-\-lan\-ip\fR \fI<IP>\fR
Local address the facade binds its HTTP service to and joins the SSDP group on. Default 192.168.21.1.
.TP
\fB\-\-upnp\-name\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Friendly name the facade reports in its device description. Default `ds\-lite\-punch IGD`.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-upnp\fR
Turn the UPnP IGD facade off. Use it when another device on the local network already answers SSDP.
.TP
\fB\-\-carrier\-probe\fR
Count the cooperating helper\*(Aqs marked probe, and raise carrier\-silent after the configured number of intervals with none. Arming this obliges the helper to run, because a helper that stopped and a carrier that stopped look the same to the counter.
.TP
\fB\-\-carrier\-probe\-interval\fR \fI<SECS>\fR
Seconds expected between the helper\*(Aqs probes. Default 900.
.TP
\fB\-\-carrier\-probe\-misses\fR \fI<N>\fR
Intervals of silence that raise carrier\-silent. Default 3.
.TP
\fB\-\-carrier\-probe\-poll\fR \fI<SECS>\fR
Seconds between readings of the counter\*(Aqs value. Default 5.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print this text and exit.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH VERSION
v0.3.5
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The service reads
.B /etc/ds-lite-punch.env
at start and turns its keys into the flags above. The defaults named here are
the shipped file's own. A key that is unset keeps the flag's default.
.TP
.B BIND
The relay socket, as \fIip:port\fR, on the CGNAT-facing address. Becomes
\fB--bind\fR.
.TP
.B TARGET
The local endpoint inbound datagrams are forwarded to. Becomes \fB--target\fR.
.TP
.B STUN
Comma-separated STUN servers. Becomes \fB--stun\fR.
.TP
.B INTERVAL
Seconds between the STUN writes. Becomes \fB--interval\fR.
.TP
.B GATEWAY
Next hop used to route STUN out the line the mapping is on. Becomes
\fB--gateway\fR.
.TP
.B UPNP_PORT
Port the facade serves on. Becomes \fB--upnp-port\fR.
.TP
.B LAN_IP
Local address the facade binds and joins SSDP on. Becomes \fB--lan-ip\fR.
.TP
.B UPNP
\fB1\fR leaves the facade on, and \fB0\fR turns it off. Becomes \fB--no-upnp\fR.
.TP
.B DEVICE_PROTECTION_ACL
Path of the DeviceProtection seed, or \fBnone\fR to disable the seed. The file
is copied into the state directory at start when no store exists yet, which is
how the first identity is created. The format is one tab-separated row per
entry, the same as the store the daemon keeps.
.TP
.B ALLOWLIST
Path of the file naming the devices whose flows are kept alive. Becomes
\fB--allowlist\fR.
.TP
.B HOLD
\fB1\fR keeps the named devices' mappings alive, and \fB0\fR leaves the
arm reporting them. Becomes \fB--keepalive\fR.
.TP
.B OBSERVATION
\fB1\fR turns the observation arm on. Becomes \fB--observation\fR.
.TP
.B PCP
\fB1\fR answers PCP and NAT-PMP on UDP 5351. Becomes \fB--pcp\fR.
.TP
.B PCP_PEER
\fB1\fR answers the PCP PEER opcode. Becomes \fB--pcp-peer\fR.
.TP
.B CARRIER_PROBE
\fB1\fR arms the carrier watch. Becomes \fB--carrier-probe\fR.
.TP
.B CARRIER_PROBE_INTERVAL
Seconds expected between the helper's probes. Becomes
\fB--carrier-probe-interval\fR.
.TP
.B CARRIER_PROBE_MISSES
Intervals of silence that raise the alarm. Becomes \fB--carrier-probe-misses\fR.
.TP
.B CARRIER_PROBE_POLL
Seconds between readings of the counter. Becomes \fB--carrier-probe-poll\fR.
.PP
Three keys are read by the service and appear in no shipped environment file,
so they are set by editing \fB/etc/init.d/ds-lite-punch\fR:
.TP
.B SLOT_RANGE
Port range the slot engine allocates from. Default \fB30000-39999\fR. Becomes
\fB--slot-port-range\fR.
.TP
.B MAX_SLOTS
Maximum concurrent slots. Default \fB32\fR. Becomes \fB--max-slots\fR.
.TP
.B MAX_MAPS_PER_CLIENT
Maximum mappings per client. Default \fB16\fR. Becomes
\fB--max-maps-per-client\fR.
.PP
The facade's friendly name has no environment key. Set \fB--upnp-name\fR on the
command line in the service script to change it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/ds-lite-punch.env
The configuration. Every key above.
.TP
.B /etc/init.d/ds-lite-punch
The procd service. It reads the configuration, seeds the DeviceProtection
store, and installs the legacy input rule.
.TP
.B /usr/bin/ds-lite-punch
The daemon.
.TP
.B /etc/ds-lite-punch.allow
The devices whose flows are kept alive, one IPv4 address per line, with # for
comments. It is installed empty, and no device's mapping is kept alive until you name one.
.TP
.B /etc/ds-lite-punch.acl
The DeviceProtection seed. It is copied to \fBdp.tsv\fR in the state directory
at start when no store exists.
.TP
.B /run/ds-lite-punch/tuple
The mapping's learned external address and port, as the carrier reports it.
Read this file to see the mapping the line currently has.
.PP
The lease records keep a path of their own, \fB/tmp/dslp\fR, and not the state
directory. The daemon writes them there on every change:
.TP
.B /tmp/dslp/epoch
One line: the unix seconds at which the lease table was first created. Read at
start to continue the PCP announce epoch across a restart.
.TP
.B /tmp/dslp/leases.tsv
One row per slot, and the record a restart re-binds its slots from.
.TP
.B /tmp/dslp/upnp.tsv
The facade's own state, including the mapping it reports to control points.
.TP
.B /run/ds-lite-punch/tuple-R
The same external tuple for the slot whose port is R, one file per slot.
.TP
.B /run/ds-lite-punch/upnp-ident
The facade's own identity, the uuid it reports to control points. It is stable
across restarts of the daemon.
.TP
.B /run/ds-lite-punch/dp.tsv
The DeviceProtection store: users and identities. The directory is on a
temporary filesystem, so this store is seeded again from
\fB/etc/ds-lite-punch.acl\fR at start when it is absent.
.SH LOG EVENTS
Every event is one line of JSON on syslog, written by the daemon and readable
with \fBlogread\fR. The event name is the value of its \fBevent\fR field.
.TP
.B start
The configuration the daemon started with: \fBbind\fR, \fBtarget\fR,
\fBstun_servers\fR and \fBslots\fR.
.TP
.B tuple
The mapping the carrier has, learned by STUN: \fBip\fR and \fBport\fR,
and \fBslot\fR when the mapping belongs to a slot.
.TP
.B upnp
The facade is serving: \fBlan\fR and the device's \fBudn\fR.
.TP
.B pcp
The PCP and NAT-PMP listener is serving: \fBbind\fR and whether PEER is
answered.
.TP
.B keepalive
The keepalive's admission and its state: \fBdevices\fR and whether its ruleset is in
force.
.TP
.B observe
The observation arm's reading: \fBcdc\fR, \fBmax_refresh_attempts\fR, \fBallowed\fR
and \fBkeepalive\fR.
.TP
.B carrier-watch
The watch is armed: \fBcounter\fR, \fBinterval\fR, \fBmisses\fR and \fBpoll\fR.
.TP
.B carrier-watch-reinstalled
The watch's counting rules were missing and have been installed again:
\fBcounter\fR and \fBepoch\fR. A firewall reload takes the rules with it and
leaves the counter object in place, so the reading looks unchanged while nothing
counts. The daemon installs them again on its next poll and says so here.
.TP
.B carrier-probe
A probe from the helper was counted: \fBcount\fR and \fBepoch\fR.
.TP
.B carrier-silent
The counter stopped rising for the configured number of intervals:
\fBlast_probe\fR, \fBwaited\fR and \fBepoch\fR. The helper and the carrier look
alike from here, so check the helper first.
.PP
Other lines carry an event name and a \fBdetail\fR field, for state a slot or a
flow passed through.
.SH LIMITS
The carrier chooses the external port. A request for a port is a key, and the
carrier may answer with a different one.
.PP
The carrier drops an idle mapping. On this line the measured windows are five
to ten seconds for UDP and 120 to 300 seconds for TCP, which is why the daemon
writes every two seconds.
.PP
One client holds one mapping on one port.
.PP
The keepalive costs about half a packet a second per flow kept alive, at the
default interval, written and reimbursed on the local side. Treat the allowlist as a
budget as well as an admission.
.PP
The daemon's rules live in two places. Its own table is not one the firewall
rebuilds. The rules it inserts into the firewall's tables are, and a reload of
the router's firewall takes them with it. The watch installs its counting rules
again on the next poll, and \fBcarrier-watch-reinstalled\fR names the moment. A
reload is otherwise a restart's business: restart the service to put the rest of
the rules back.
